1
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:08,630
This video shows you how to add a worker on Kubernetes that keeps its identity when its pod restarts.

2
00:00:08,680 --> 00:00:15,375
Open a new file, smeltr-worker.yaml, and paste in the worker's manifest from the guide.

3
00:00:20,280 --> 00:00:23,664
Its config map says which group the worker joins.

4
00:00:23,664 --> 00:00:25,550
Here, the default group.

5
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:28,741
Change the leader's address to yours.

6
00:00:31,120 --> 00:00:33,913
This secret holds the enrollment code.

7
00:00:33,913 --> 00:00:35,190
Mint one now.

8
00:00:35,240 --> 00:00:40,660
In smeltr, open Admin, then Workers, then Enrollment codes.

9
00:00:41,560 --> 00:00:44,358
Add an enrollment code for the default group.

10
00:00:44,358 --> 00:00:47,072
It brings in one permanent worker.

11
00:00:47,800 --> 00:00:50,437
Mint it, and copy the code.

12
00:00:54,440 --> 00:00:58,252
Back in the terminal, paste the code into the secret.

13
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:02,685
The stateful set runs one worker.

14
00:01:02,685 --> 00:01:07,390
For more, raise replicas, and mint a code that brings in that many.

15
00:01:07,440 --> 00:01:12,190
The long grace period gives a stopping worker time to finish its work cleanly.

16
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:18,457
Name the image by the same digest as the leader's, read in from digest.txt.

17
00:01:19,120 --> 00:01:23,195
The worker's thread limit comes from its CPU request, just below.

18
00:01:23,195 --> 00:01:27,990
Without a thread limit, it would run a thread for every core on its node.

19
00:01:28,040 --> 00:01:30,611
Size the memory for what the group runs.

20
00:01:30,611 --> 00:01:32,990
The guide's sizing page shows how.

21
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:35,760
Its identity lives in its own volume.

22
00:01:35,760 --> 00:01:38,350
Never share or copy that volume.

23
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:40,270
Save the file.

24
00:01:40,320 --> 00:01:43,262
Apply it, and wait for the pod.

25
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:57,030
In Status, the default group has one worker, and it's online.

26
00:01:57,080 --> 00:01:59,610
The worker needs its code only to enroll.

27
00:01:59,610 --> 00:02:03,630
From then on, it reconnects with the credential in its volume.

28
00:02:03,680 --> 00:02:04,802
Delete its pod.

29
00:02:04,802 --> 00:02:09,517
The stateful set starts it again, with the same volume.

30
00:02:15,760 --> 00:02:19,870
Back in Status, it's the same worker, online again.

31
00:02:19,920 --> 00:02:23,358
This code is spent, so it enrolls no one else.

32
00:02:23,358 --> 00:02:29,040
A code with uses left is a live secret, so guard it like a password.

33
00:02:30,440 --> 00:02:32,246
Check on learning.

34
00:02:32,446 --> 00:02:37,102
When the worker's pod restarts, how does it reconnect?

35
00:02:41,502 --> 00:02:45,296
With the credential it keeps in its own volume.

36
00:02:46,196 --> 00:02:49,252
Next, you autoscale workers.
